     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Dear list,<div><br><div>I have been playing with a model that models a distance between animals as a function of time (tsv). A tentative model is listed below, and includes a fixed factor (type) and a random effect for animal pair (pid).</div><div><br></div><div><pre style=\"box-sizing:border-box;overflow:auto;padding:9.5px;line-height:1.42857;word-break:break-all;word-wrap:break-word;color:rgb(51,51,51);background-color:rgb(245,245,245);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-radius:4px\"><code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-size:inherit;color:inherit;background-color:transparent;border-radius:0px;white-space:pre-wrap\">inlamod1=<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(0,112,32);font-weight:bold\">inla</span>(<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(144,32,0)\">formula=</span>dxyz~type+<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(0,112,32);font-weight:bold\">f</span>(pid,<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(144,32,0)\">model=</span><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(64,112,160)\">&quot;iid&quot;</span>)+<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(0,112,32);font-weight:bold\">f</span>(tsv,<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(144,32,0)\">model=</span><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(64,112,160)\">&quot;rw1&quot;</span>),<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(144,32,0)\">data=</span>inladat)</code></pre></div><div><br></div><div>This is what the smooth term plot looks like - and it seemed to be overfitting to me (as compared to say what a vanilla GAM would)</div><div><br></div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-e5bE6zHO40M/Wrjk3ebE1EI/AAAAAAAAYFc/xmqI2pdqf6s0lQL5keGN0S9OHCdDpxgPACLcBGAs/s1600/tsv1.JPG\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-e5bE6zHO40M/Wrjk3ebE1EI/AAAAAAAAYFc/xmqI2pdqf6s0lQL5keGN0S9OHCdDpxgPACLcBGAs/s1600/tsv1.JPG&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792443481606000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2r_Nf1gKNKrNuQC95pxlOh\"><img src=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-e5bE6zHO40M/Wrjk3ebE1EI/AAAAAAAAYFc/xmqI2pdqf6s0lQL5keGN0S9OHCdDpxgPACLcBGAs/s320/tsv1.JPG\" border=\"0\" width=\"307\" height=\"320\"></a></p><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>I read in <a href=\"https://www.r-bloggers.com/smoothing-a-time-series-with-a-bayesian-model/\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(21,122,181);text-decoration-line:underline;outline:0px;font-family:&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.r-bloggers.com/smoothing-a-time-series-with-a-bayesian-model/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792443481606000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0TnTTz6nd2ni03oKlPRHHP\">https://www.r-bloggers.com/smoothing-a-time-series-with-a-bayesian-model/</a>  how one might go about tweaking the degree of smoothness of the rw1. To do so, I used the following syntax:</div><div><br></div><div><pre style=\"box-sizing:border-box;overflow:auto;padding:9.5px;line-height:1.42857;word-break:break-all;word-wrap:break-word;color:rgb(51,51,51);background-color:rgb(245,245,245);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-radius:4px\"><code style=\"box-sizing:border-box;font-size:inherit;color:inherit;background-color:transparent;border-radius:0px;white-space:pre-wrap\">inlamod1a005=<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(0,112,32);font-weight:bold\">inla</span>(<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(144,32,0)\">formula=</span>dxyz~type+<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(0,112,32);font-weight:bold\">f</span>(pid,<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(144,32,0)\">model=</span><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(64,112,160)\">&quot;iid&quot;</span>)+ <span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(0,112,32);font-weight:bold\">f</span>(tsv,<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(144,32,0)\">model=</span><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(64,112,160)\">&quot;rw1&quot;</span>, <span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(144,32,0)\">scale.model =</span> <span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(0,112,32)\">TRUE</span>,<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(144,32,0)\">hyper =</span> <span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(0,112,32);font-weight:bold\">list</span>(<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(144,32,0)\">theta =</span> <span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(0,112,32);font-weight:bold\">list</span>(<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(144,32,0)\">prior=</span><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(64,112,160)\">&#39;pc.prec&#39;</span>, <span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(144,32,0)\">param=</span><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(0,112,32);font-weight:bold\">c</span>(<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(64,160,112)\">X</span>,<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(64,160,112)\">0.01</span>)))),<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(144,32,0)\">data=</span>inladat)</code></pre></div><div><br></div><div>where by trial and error I used several values of X <span style=\"color:rgb(85,85,85);font-family:&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\">(0.05,1,2,2.5,2.65, 2.75,3,4,5)</span>. The results are surprising to me, as I actually can&#39;t control the smooth. The smooth behaviour is on-off pattern: it is either the same as I got without specifying the prior explicitly (see plot above, for values <span style=\"color:rgb(85,85,85);font-family:&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\">0.05,1,2,2.5,2.65</span>) or a horizontal line (see plot below, for values<span style=\"color:rgb(85,85,85);font-family:&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(85,85,85);font-family:&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\">2.75,3,4,5</span>), so no varying degrees of smoothness. Can someone explain what might be going on and how one would effectively tune the degree of smoothness</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-JH9dbMnMmCs/WrjbWRCw5QI/AAAAAAAAYFI/TEKmmy7hrqQKLmYqYk3YrUe--7EGFKWZACLcBGAs/s1600/tsv2.JPG\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-JH9dbMnMmCs/WrjbWRCw5QI/AAAAAAAAYFI/TEKmmy7hrqQKLmYqYk3YrUe--7EGFKWZACLcBGAs/s1600/tsv2.JPG&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792443481606000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3Ge02AJ4Q8z1p-VSREOrYF\"><img src=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-JH9dbMnMmCs/WrjbWRCw5QI/AAAAAAAAYFI/TEKmmy7hrqQKLmYqYk3YrUe--7EGFKWZACLcBGAs/s320/tsv2.JPG\" border=\"0\" width=\"315\" height=\"320\"></a></p><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\">Many thanks</p><span><font color=\"#888888\"><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\">Tiago</p><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"></p><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><br></p><div><br></div></font></span></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
