   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] Controlling smoothness in a rw1 - surprising results.",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">If you want to control the smoothness &quot;manually&quot;, I would do this by fixing the hyper-parameters to different values. They control the signal-to-noise ratios, resulting in different &quot;smoothness look&quot;. More noise usually means smoother signal. More differentiability (rw2) means smoother signal. These two types of smoothness are completely different concepts.<div><br></div><div>See for example the shiny app you can download at</div><div><a href=\"https://haakonbakka.bitbucket.io/btopic115.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://haakonbakka.bitbucket.io/btopic115.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792443481607000&amp;usg=AOvVaw186fsxaM0Z4CpZlTI6zPDi\">https://haakonbakka.bitbucket.io/btopic115.html</a><br></div><div>for fixing different hyperparameter values. (This webpage is only a draft version)</div><div><br></div><div>Regards,</div><div>Haakon</div><div><br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
